WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.765
Getting ready for school is a big
job. Break it into named parts.

2
00:00:08.985 --> 00:00:13.304
Each part is a sub-routine: a
small algorithm with a name.

3
00:00:13.804 --> 00:00:18.820
A big task is easier in parts.
Here is one: a school morning.

4
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.752
Decomposition breaks it into four
parts: wash, dress, breakfast and bag.

5
00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:28.873
In an algorithm each part becomes
a sub-routine with a name.

6
00:00:29.093 --> 00:00:34.108
A sub-routine is a small algorithm.
WASH is face, teeth and hair.

7
00:00:34.328 --> 00:00:37.533
It skips nothing. All the
steps are still there.

8
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:41.098
The main algorithm is the
short list at the top.

9
00:00:41.318 --> 00:00:44.847
It has one line for each part,
in the order they happen.

10
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:49.850
The first line is do WASH. Then
do DRESS, do BREAKFAST and do BAG.

11
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.668
Then leave the house. That is five
lines you can read at a glance.

12
00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:58.975
Each do is a call. A call runs
the sub-routine with that name.

13
00:00:59.195 --> 00:01:02.910
Behind those five lines sits
all the detail, tucked away.

14
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.868
Now follow it. You reach do WASH,
so jump into that sub-routine.

15
00:01:08.088 --> 00:01:11.896
Do every one of its steps:
face, then teeth, then hair.

16
00:01:12.116 --> 00:01:15.739
Then jump back to the line
after the call, and carry on.

17
00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:21.810
The next line is do DRESS, so jump in
again: shirt, trousers, socks, shoes.

18
00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.395
Back once more, on through BREAKFAST
and BAG, then leave the house.

19
00:01:26.615 --> 00:01:31.120
Five lines did every step in
the parts. Nothing was skipped.

20
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:38.168
Bedtime has a main algorithm too:
pyjamas, do WASH, do STORY, lights off.

21
00:01:38.388 --> 00:01:43.961
There is do WASH again. The same
sub-routine, in a different routine.

22
00:01:44.181 --> 00:01:49.243
Both lists point at the same box
of steps, written out only once.

23
00:01:49.463 --> 00:01:52.806
It is called in the morning,
and called again at bedtime.

24
00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:58.228
One copy of the steps. Two calls
to it. That is what reuse means.

25
00:01:58.728 --> 00:02:03.186
Now add flossing to the WASH
sub-routine, and watch both routines.

26
00:02:03.406 --> 00:02:06.982
Both of them get flossing, and
you edited neither of them.

27
00:02:07.202 --> 00:02:12.821
You change one list, not three. That
is three chances not to miss one.

28
00:02:13.041 --> 00:02:17.221
Sub-routines share the work
too. One person writes WASH.

29
00:02:17.441 --> 00:02:21.063
Another writes DRESS, and the
main algorithm joins them.

30
00:02:21.563 --> 00:02:25.650
Decomposition breaks one big
task into smaller named parts.

31
00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.978
Each part is a sub-routine, and the
main algorithm calls them in order.

32
00:02:31.198 --> 00:02:35.006
A call jumps into a sub-routine.
A return brings you back.

33
00:02:35.226 --> 00:02:38.941
Written once, used many times,
and changed in one place.
